    },
    {
      "id": "waESElUWg_4ZTw42OYwXs",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -5958.890412675386,
      "y": 7825.053068522463,
      "width": 872.3794555664062,
      "height": 700,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": "N_7VHjFYCuz-m-bbYgyMX",
      "index": "a6",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1737796432,
      "version": 1926,
      "versionNonce": 750209427,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785262697953,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "ch08 data distribution shifts and monitoring\n\nSoftware vs ML-specific failures\n> dependency (packaging version)\n> deployment\n> hardware\n> crashing\n\nML Specific\n> training vs production\n    > real world is continously changing \n    > bugs in pipeline: missing/incorect data, wrong subset\n> edge cases (the <1%)\n    > degenerate feedback loops\n        > homogenous where predictions affect user data\n        >> course correct hetereogenous through randomization and positional features\n\n** distribution shift =/= concept drift **\n    > covariate shift\n    > occasionally label\n\nMonitoring and Observaility\nMonitoring: tracking, measure, logging of metrics\nObservability: visbility (i.e frtonend)\n\n** 4 major concerns for monitoring **\n1. a company might have 100s of models in production each uses 100s, 1000s of features\n    > computing monitoring alone at this scale is computatiaonlly intensive ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "ch08 data distribution shifts and monitoring\n\nSoftware vs ML-specific failures\n> dependency (packaging version)\n> deployment\n> hardware\n> crashing\n\nML Specific\n> training vs production\n    > real world is continously changing \n    > bugs in pipeline: missing/incorect data, wrong subset\n> edge cases (the <1%)\n    > degenerate feedback loops\n        > homogenous where predictions affect user data\n        >> course correct hetereogenous through randomization and positional features\n\n** distribution shift =/= concept drift **\n    > covariate shift\n    > occasionally label\n\nMonitoring and Observaility\nMonitoring: tracking, measure, logging of metrics\nObservability: visbility (i.e frtonend)\n\n** 4 major concerns for monitoring **\n1. a company might have 100s of models in production each uses 100s, 1000s of features\n    > computing monitoring alone at this scale is computatiaonlly intensive ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "yBnWev7huhIs2QMO0G5R9",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -6021.263111991584,
      "y": 8595.057728761043,
      "width": 1987.5787353515625,
      "height": 525,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": "N_7VHjFYCuz-m-bbYgyMX",
      "index": "a7",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 540231504,
      "version": 2349,
      "versionNonce": 80197982,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785292332773,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "ch09 continual learning and test in production\n\nif a model is a neural network, if trained too frequently, tends to forget (weight adjustments over time)\nif retrained too frequently, hardware backends designed for batch vs. continous batch training -- could be a waste of compute if not significant\n> blue-green deployment\n> canary deployment / roll out\n\nStateless vs stateful training\n    > stateless: retraining from scratch each time\n    > stateful: training ontop of new data\n\n4 stages of continous learning\n1. manual, stateless training\n    > try to solve as many ML problems as possible, uipdating existing takes a back burner focusing on ad-hoc manual tuning\n2. autoated retraining (runners, schedulers, data, mdoel store)\n    > priortity shifts to maintaining existing models\n3. automated stateful training\n    > loads previous checkout into memory before continous training; infrastucture must support stateful training (model and data async training end-to-end requirements)\n4. continual learning\n    > finding the optimal schedule isn't straight forward and can be situation dependent; anomolies in the real world affect data and models need to be retrained to reflect this otherwise nothing happens\n    > consider retraining when performance drops",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "ch09 continual learning and test in production\n\nif a model is a neural network, if trained too frequently, tends to forget (weight adjustments over time)\nif retrained too frequently, hardware backends designed for batch vs. continous batch training -- could be a waste of compute if not significant\n> blue-green deployment\n> canary deployment / roll out\n\nStateless vs stateful training\n    > stateless: retraining from scratch each time\n    > stateful: training ontop of new data\n\n4 stages of continous learning\n1. manual, stateless training\n    > try to solve as many ML problems as possible, uipdating existing takes a back burner focusing on ad-hoc manual tuning\n2. autoated retraining (runners, schedulers, data, mdoel store)\n    > priortity shifts to maintaining existing models\n3. automated stateful training\n    > loads previous checkout into memory before continous training; infrastucture must support stateful training (model and data async training end-to-end requirements)\n4. continual learning\n    > finding the optimal schedule isn't straight forward and can be situation dependent; anomolies in the real world affect data and models need to be retrained to reflect this otherwise nothing happens\n    > consider retraining when performance drops",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "OG8KGJry9MYpOzh14uniG",
      "type": "text",
