    },
    {
      "id": "vFfNvCc5Zg_M0t9EYqnOe",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -3647.21347218524,
      "y": -1408.019242833173,
      "width": 1445.0106201171868,
      "height": 3025,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": "Yjzyuw87AGawBWzT_o8kX",
      "index": "aXt",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1658162608,
      "version": 3112,
      "versionNonce": 830567344,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1784601284406,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Table Of Content\n● Introduction to Relational Databases\n    - structured data\n        - entities, attributes, and relationships\n    - normalized\n    \n● ACID Properties: reliability of transactions in a relaitonal database management system (RDBMS)\n    - atomicity: transactions are all or nothing\n    - consistency: data remains after a transaction\n    - isolation: transactions do not interfere with each other\n    - durability: once a transaction is committed, it remains committed even in case of failure\n\n● Differences between SQL & NoSQL\n    - SQL: structured data with a fixed schema, strong ACID compliance, support for complex queries and transactions\n    - NoSQL: flexible schema, horizontal scalibility where performance and flexibility are prioritized over consistency\n        - BASE properties; basically available soft state, is often eventual consistency\n            - Key-Value (Redis), Document-Based (MongoDB), Column-Based (Cassandra), Graph-based\n                - in system design, characterize by type of NoSQL database not the implementation level (these are just examples) without having\nalternatives and trade offs known + provider alternatives\n\n● Vector Databases\n    - vector supported vs. dedicated vector databases: vectors are outputs from an embedding model enabling semantic simiarltiy searches by\nconverting complex objects into numerical vectors\n        - 2026: industry standard is hybrid retrieval augmented generation using classical RAG + agentic RAG\n        - uses cosine similarity upon querying the input text with the existing database store of vectors to evaluate, top (25) results ranked\n\n● Relational Data Model\n    - 1 to 1\n    - 1 to many\n    - many to 1\n    - many to many\n\n● Keys in Relational Databases\n    - primary keys\n        - candidate keys and super keys\n    - foreign keys\n\n* Data Intengrity and Constraints; maintaining data quality\n    - NOT NULL\n    - UNIQUE\n    - CHECK\n    - DEFAULT\n\n● Database Design Process\n    1. Requirements Gathering: Understand the needs of the users and the system.\n        ● Customer Needs: Understand product selection, search capabilities,\n        and ordering options.\n        ● Inventory Management: Define how inventory tracking should work.\n        ● Payment and Checkout: Specify payment methods and security\n        measures.\n        ● User Accounts: Identify required information for customer profiles.\n        ● Delivery: Clarify delivery preferences and scheduling options.\n\n    2. Conceptual Design: Create an abstract model of the database using entities, attributes, and relationships.\n        ● Entities: Identify primary entities like \"Products,\" \"Customers,\" \"Orders,\" and \"Inventory.\"\n        ● Attributes: Define attributes for each entity.\n        ● Relationships: Establish connections between entities.\n\n    3. Logical Design: Refine the conceptual model into detailed tables, relationships, and constraints.\n        ● Tables: Create tables for each entity, specifying fields.\n        ● Data Types: Define data types for fields.\n        ● Constraints: Implement primary keys, foreign keys, and unique constraints.\n        ● Normalization: Optimize the structure, reducing redundancy.\n\n    4. Physical Design: Implement the database in a DBMS, optimizing for performance and security.\n        ● DBMS Selection: Choose a suitable DBMS like MySQL, PostgreSQL.\n        ● Indexing: Determine which fields require indexing for faster queries.\n        ● Partitioning: Consider partitioning strategies for large datasets.\n        ● Security: Implement security measures to protect data.\n        ● Performance Tuning: Optimize database performance through query optimization.\n\n● Entity-Relationship Diagrams\n        Introduction to ERD\n        Visual tools that provide a graphical representation of a database’s\n        structure, showing entities, attributes, and relationships.\n        Components of an ERD\n        ● Entities: Represent real-world objects or concepts.\n        ● Attributes: Properties or characteristics of entities.\n        ● Relationships: Establish connections between entities.\n        ● Cardinality: Defines the number of related records between entities.\n\n● Normalization Techniques\n    Normalization is a database design technique that helps us organize data\n    efficiently, reducing redundancy and ensuring data integrity.\n    \n    Levels of Normalization\n        ● First Normal Form (1NF): This is the most basic level of\n        normalization. In 1NF, each table cell should contain only a single\n        value, and each column should have a unique name. The first normal\n        form helps to eliminate duplicate data and simplify queries.\n    \n        ● Second Normal Form (2NF): 2NF eliminates redundant data by\n        requiring that each non-key attribute be dependent on the primary\n        key. This means that each column should be directly related to the\n        primary key, and not to other columns.\n    \n        ● Third Normal Form (3NF): 3NF builds on 2NF by requiring that all\n        non-key attributes are independent of each other. This means that\n        each column should be directly related to the primary key, and not to\n        any other columns in the same table.\n    \n        ● Boyce-Codd Normal Form (BCNF): BCNF is a stricter form of 3NF\n        that ensures that each determinant in a table is a candidate key. In\n        other words, BCNF ensures that each non-key attribute is dependent\n        only on the candidate key.\n    \n    Normal forms help to reduce data redundancy, increase data consistency,\n    and improve database performance. However, higher levels of\n    normalization can lead to more complex database designs and queries. It is\n    important to strike a balance between normalization and practicality when\n    designing a database.\n    \nDatabase Security and Authorization\n    Key Areas of Focus\n    1. Data Encryption: Encrypt data at rest and in transit using SSL/TLS and AES.\n    2. Strong Authentication: Implement multi-factor authentication (MFA) and secure password storage.\n    3. Authorization and RBAC: Define roles and assign permissions accordingly.\n    4. Audit Trails and Logging: Monitor database activity and retain logs for forensic analysis.\n    5. Backup and Disaster Recovery: Regularly backup data and establish recovery plans.\n    6. Network Security: Use firewalls, IDS/IPS, and network monitoring to protect the database.\n ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Table Of Content\n● Introduction to Relational Databases\n    - structured data\n        - entities, attributes, and relationships\n    - normalized\n    \n● ACID Properties: reliability of transactions in a relaitonal database management system (RDBMS)\n    - atomicity: transactions are all or nothing\n    - consistency: data remains after a transaction\n    - isolation: transactions do not interfere with each other\n    - durability: once a transaction is committed, it remains committed even in case of failure\n\n● Differences between SQL & NoSQL\n    - SQL: structured data with a fixed schema, strong ACID compliance, support for complex queries and transactions\n    - NoSQL: flexible schema, horizontal scalibility where performance and flexibility are prioritized over consistency\n        - BASE properties; basically available soft state, is often eventual consistency\n            - Key-Value (Redis), Document-Based (MongoDB), Column-Based (Cassandra), Graph-based\n                - in system design, characterize by type of NoSQL database not the implementation level (these are just examples) without having alternatives and trade offs known + provider alternatives\n\n● Vector Databases\n    - vector supported vs. dedicated vector databases: vectors are outputs from an embedding model enabling semantic simiarltiy searches by converting complex objects into numerical vectors\n        - 2026: industry standard is hybrid retrieval augmented generation using classical RAG + agentic RAG\n        - uses cosine similarity upon querying the input text with the existing database store of vectors to evaluate, top (25) results ranked\n\n● Relational Data Model\n    - 1 to 1\n    - 1 to many\n    - many to 1\n    - many to many\n\n● Keys in Relational Databases\n    - primary keys\n        - candidate keys and super keys\n    - foreign keys\n\n* Data Intengrity and Constraints; maintaining data quality\n    - NOT NULL\n    - UNIQUE\n    - CHECK\n    - DEFAULT\n\n● Database Design Process\n    1. Requirements Gathering: Understand the needs of the users and the system.\n        ● Customer Needs: Understand product selection, search capabilities,\n        and ordering options.\n        ● Inventory Management: Define how inventory tracking should work.\n        ● Payment and Checkout: Specify payment methods and security\n        measures.\n        ● User Accounts: Identify required information for customer profiles.\n        ● Delivery: Clarify delivery preferences and scheduling options.\n\n    2. Conceptual Design: Create an abstract model of the database using entities, attributes, and relationships.\n        ● Entities: Identify primary entities like \"Products,\" \"Customers,\" \"Orders,\" and \"Inventory.\"\n        ● Attributes: Define attributes for each entity.\n        ● Relationships: Establish connections between entities.\n\n    3. Logical Design: Refine the conceptual model into detailed tables, relationships, and constraints.\n        ● Tables: Create tables for each entity, specifying fields.\n        ● Data Types: Define data types for fields.\n        ● Constraints: Implement primary keys, foreign keys, and unique constraints.\n        ● Normalization: Optimize the structure, reducing redundancy.\n\n    4. Physical Design: Implement the database in a DBMS, optimizing for performance and security.\n        ● DBMS Selection: Choose a suitable DBMS like MySQL, PostgreSQL.\n        ● Indexing: Determine which fields require indexing for faster queries.\n        ● Partitioning: Consider partitioning strategies for large datasets.\n        ● Security: Implement security measures to protect data.\n        ● Performance Tuning: Optimize database performance through query optimization.\n\n● Entity-Relationship Diagrams\n        Introduction to ERD\n        Visual tools that provide a graphical representation of a database’s\n        structure, showing entities, attributes, and relationships.\n        Components of an ERD\n        ● Entities: Represent real-world objects or concepts.\n        ● Attributes: Properties or characteristics of entities.\n        ● Relationships: Establish connections between entities.\n        ● Cardinality: Defines the number of related records between entities.\n\n● Normalization Techniques\n    Normalization is a database design technique that helps us organize data\n    efficiently, reducing redundancy and ensuring data integrity.\n    \n    Levels of Normalization\n        ● First Normal Form (1NF): This is the most basic level of\n        normalization. In 1NF, each table cell should contain only a single\n        value, and each column should have a unique name. The first normal\n        form helps to eliminate duplicate data and simplify queries.\n    \n        ● Second Normal Form (2NF): 2NF eliminates redundant data by\n        requiring that each non-key attribute be dependent on the primary\n        key. This means that each column should be directly related to the\n        primary key, and not to other columns.\n    \n        ● Third Normal Form (3NF): 3NF builds on 2NF by requiring that all\n        non-key attributes are independent of each other. This means that\n        each column should be directly related to the primary key, and not to\n        any other columns in the same table.\n    \n        ● Boyce-Codd Normal Form (BCNF): BCNF is a stricter form of 3NF\n        that ensures that each determinant in a table is a candidate key. In\n        other words, BCNF ensures that each non-key attribute is dependent\n        only on the candidate key.\n    \n    Normal forms help to reduce data redundancy, increase data consistency,\n    and improve database performance. However, higher levels of\n    normalization can lead to more complex database designs and queries. It is\n    important to strike a balance between normalization and practicality when\n    designing a database.\n    \nDatabase Security and Authorization\n    Key Areas of Focus\n    1. Data Encryption: Encrypt data at rest and in transit using SSL/TLS and AES.\n    2. Strong Authentication: Implement multi-factor authentication (MFA) and secure password storage.\n    3. Authorization and RBAC: Define roles and assign permissions accordingly.\n    4. Audit Trails and Logging: Monitor database activity and retain logs for forensic analysis.\n    5. Backup and Disaster Recovery: Regularly backup data and establish recovery plans.\n    6. Network Security: Use firewalls, IDS/IPS, and network monitoring to protect the database.\n ",
