    {
      "id": "3Q_ohdH7WWl0WC7lrEcjR",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -7645.722062802701,
      "y": 10922.439535388292,
      "width": 1986.9384765625,
      "height": 547.8409749734277,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": "3EdYPM773z8YdaU7vCrUJ",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1323070305,
      "version": 97,
      "versionNonce": 866517651,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785261825821,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- Treat an ML system design interview as generic system design PLUS an ML-specific axis: even if not asked, proactively surface training/serving skew, data + label lineage, model versioning, and retraining cadence early in your answer.\n- Use the pipeline as your answer skeleton (Chain of Responsibility): Data -> Features -> Training -> Evaluation -> Deployment -> Monitoring -> Retraining. Don't lecture through every stage; use it to structure scope-narrowing questions.\n- Online vs batch prediction is a first framing decision, not an afterthought:\n  - Online: synchronous, low-latency, needs live feature lookups (feature store), for interactive user-facing decisions.\n  - Batch: precomputed on a schedule, higher throughput, staler by definition.\n  - Many \"real-time\" products are hybrid: batch-precompute + online fallback/re-rank.\n- Deployment strategies escalate in blast radius and each catches different failure classes:\n  - Shadow deployment: new model runs in parallel, predictions logged not served -> zero user risk, tests infra/latency/crashes, not quality.\n  - Canary: small % of real traffic, ramp gradually -> catches infra + basic quality regressions.\n  - A/B test: statistical comparison on a business metric, needs a defined randomization unit + guardrail metrics -> catches business-impact regressions.\n- Prefer shadow -> canary -> A/B in that order for any model launch; skipping shadow means your canary traffic is your crash test.\n- Champion-challenger is a continuous, ongoing pattern (not just a one-time launch gate) — always have a current \"champion\" in prod and challengers evaluated against it on a rolling basis.\n- Training-serving skew is the single most common real-world ML production bug:\n  - Causes: separate code paths computing features offline (batch/training) vs online (serving), time-window mismatches, point-in-time label leakage in the training pipeline.\n  - Mitigation: shared feature-transformation code, and a feature store as the single source of truth for both training and serving.\n- Monitoring must distinguish three distinct failure modes, and you should name all three explicitly in an interview:\n  - Data drift: input feature distribution shifts (P(x) changes).\n  - Concept drift: the input-output relationship changes (P(y|x) changes — same input, different correct answer over time).\n  - Model/label staleness: model was correct at training time but the world has moved on and ground truth is simply old.\n- Don't rely on raw accuracy as your primary alert — ground truth labels are often delayed or unavailable in real time. Use leading proxy signals instead: prediction distribution shift, model confidence/score drift, feature distribution monitors (e.g. PSI/KL divergence), and downstream business-metric deltas.\n- Continual learning needs explicit, named retraining triggers — don't leave this implicit:\n  - Scheduled (fixed cadence, e.g. daily/weekly).\n  - Performance-based (a monitored metric crosses a threshold).\n  - Data-volume-based (N new labeled examples accumulated).\n- Full retrain-from-scratch vs incremental fine-tune from the last checkpoint is a real cost/risk tradeoff: incremental is cheaper but risks compounding drift and catastrophic forgetting — mention a periodic full-retrain \"reset\" as a safeguard.\n- Online learning (updating weights directly on live traffic) is high-risk — call out degenerate feedback loops and adversarial/gaming behavior (bad actors learning to manipulate the model) as a known failure mode, and always pair it with a rollback plan / kill switch.\n- Use precise MLOps infra vocabulary rather than generic data-engineering terms:\n  - Feature store: guarantees offline/online consistency for the same feature logic.\n  - Model registry: versioning, staging->production promotion, and lineage back to the exact training data + code commit + hyperparameters that produced a given model.\n  - Pipeline orchestration for ML is distinct from generic ETL DAGs: it must version data + model + code together as one deployable unit (\"model = data + code + config\").\n- A model registry's real interview-answer value is rollback: always state explicitly which model is in prod, what produced it, and how you roll back to the previous version — treat rollback as a first-class deployment feature, not an afterthought.\n- Batch prediction pipelines need monitoring beyond job exit codes — a job can succeed (exit 0) while silently producing empty, truncated, or stale output; monitor row counts, schema, and freshness of the output artifact itself.\n- Ground every scale claim in QPS, latency budget, and feature-freshness requirement, then work backward to online vs batch vs hybrid and whether a feature store is even justified — proposing a feature store at low scale/simple feature sets is a common interview red flag (over-engineering).",
      "fontSize": 13.280993332689157,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- Treat an ML system design interview as generic system design PLUS an ML-specific axis: even if not asked, proactively surface training/serving skew, data + label lineage, model versioning, and retraining cadence early in your answer.\n- Use the pipeline as your answer skeleton (Chain of Responsibility): Data -> Features -> Training -> Evaluation -> Deployment -> Monitoring -> Retraining. Don't lecture through every stage; use it to structure scope-narrowing questions.\n- Online vs batch prediction is a first framing decision, not an afterthought:\n  - Online: synchronous, low-latency, needs live feature lookups (feature store), for interactive user-facing decisions.\n  - Batch: precomputed on a schedule, higher throughput, staler by definition.\n  - Many \"real-time\" products are hybrid: batch-precompute + online fallback/re-rank.\n- Deployment strategies escalate in blast radius and each catches different failure classes:\n  - Shadow deployment: new model runs in parallel, predictions logged not served -> zero user risk, tests infra/latency/crashes, not quality.\n  - Canary: small % of real traffic, ramp gradually -> catches infra + basic quality regressions.\n  - A/B test: statistical comparison on a business metric, needs a defined randomization unit + guardrail metrics -> catches business-impact regressions.\n- Prefer shadow -> canary -> A/B in that order for any model launch; skipping shadow means your canary traffic is your crash test.\n- Champion-challenger is a continuous, ongoing pattern (not just a one-time launch gate) — always have a current \"champion\" in prod and challengers evaluated against it on a rolling basis.\n- Training-serving skew is the single most common real-world ML production bug:\n  - Causes: separate code paths computing features offline (batch/training) vs online (serving), time-window mismatches, point-in-time label leakage in the training pipeline.\n  - Mitigation: shared feature-transformation code, and a feature store as the single source of truth for both training and serving.\n- Monitoring must distinguish three distinct failure modes, and you should name all three explicitly in an interview:\n  - Data drift: input feature distribution shifts (P(x) changes).\n  - Concept drift: the input-output relationship changes (P(y|x) changes — same input, different correct answer over time).\n  - Model/label staleness: model was correct at training time but the world has moved on and ground truth is simply old.\n- Don't rely on raw accuracy as your primary alert — ground truth labels are often delayed or unavailable in real time. Use leading proxy signals instead: prediction distribution shift, model confidence/score drift, feature distribution monitors (e.g. PSI/KL divergence), and downstream business-metric deltas.\n- Continual learning needs explicit, named retraining triggers — don't leave this implicit:\n  - Scheduled (fixed cadence, e.g. daily/weekly).\n  - Performance-based (a monitored metric crosses a threshold).\n  - Data-volume-based (N new labeled examples accumulated).\n- Full retrain-from-scratch vs incremental fine-tune from the last checkpoint is a real cost/risk tradeoff: incremental is cheaper but risks compounding drift and catastrophic forgetting — mention a periodic full-retrain \"reset\" as a safeguard.\n- Online learning (updating weights directly on live traffic) is high-risk — call out degenerate feedback loops and adversarial/gaming behavior (bad actors learning to manipulate the model) as a known failure mode, and always pair it with a rollback plan / kill switch.\n- Use precise MLOps infra vocabulary rather than generic data-engineering terms:\n  - Feature store: guarantees offline/online consistency for the same feature logic.\n  - Model registry: versioning, staging->production promotion, and lineage back to the exact training data + code commit + hyperparameters that produced a given model.\n  - Pipeline orchestration for ML is distinct from generic ETL DAGs: it must version data + model + code together as one deployable unit (\"model = data + code + config\").\n- A model registry's real interview-answer value is rollback: always state explicitly which model is in prod, what produced it, and how you roll back to the previous version — treat rollback as a first-class deployment feature, not an afterthought.\n- Batch prediction pipelines need monitoring beyond job exit codes — a job can succeed (exit 0) while silently producing empty, truncated, or stale output; monitor row counts, schema, and freshness of the output artifact itself.\n- Ground every scale claim in QPS, latency budget, and feature-freshness requirement, then work backward to online vs batch vs hybrid and whether a feature store is even justified — proposing a feature store at low scale/simple feature sets is a common interview red flag (over-engineering).",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "index": "az"
    },
