    {
      "id": "6v6VC0iFP9ZIq1OxoAwsB",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -7296.882368146212,
      "y": 12050.439535388292,
      "width": 632,
      "height": 670,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": "TamhnL8CNdT36hL_ntkZx",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 725477212,
      "version": 2,
      "versionNonce": 1762821053,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785258477418,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- \"Machine Learning Design Patterns\" — Lakshmanan, Robinson & Munn (O'Reilly, 2020) — catalog of recurring ML engineering patterns (e.g. feature store, checkpoints, cascade).\n- \"Rules of Machine Learning: Best Practices for ML Engineering\" — Martin Zinkevich, Google (developers.google.com) — terse, battle-tested production ML rules from Google.\n- Google Cloud Architecture Center: \"MLOps: Continuous delivery and automation pipelines in machine learning\" — reference architecture for CI/CD/CT maturity levels in ML systems.\n- Stanford CS 329S: Machine Learning Systems Design (Chip Huyen) — public course notes/syllabus; the course this board's core book grew out of.\n- Full Stack Deep Learning (fullstackdeeplearning.com) — production-focused course covering deployment, monitoring, and infra for ML teams.\n- Made With ML (madewithml.com, Goku Mohandas) — applied MLOps lessons with runnable code, from data to deployment.\n- Eugene Yan — applyingml.com — applied ML system design write-ups from practitioners at Amazon/Alibaba and other large-scale teams.\n- Evidently AI blog (evidentlyai.com) — deep dives specifically on ML monitoring, data drift, and concept drift detection in production.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- \"Machine Learning Design Patterns\" — Lakshmanan, Robinson & Munn (O'Reilly, 2020) — catalog of recurring ML engineering patterns (e.g. feature store, checkpoints, cascade).\n- \"Rules of Machine Learning: Best Practices for ML Engineering\" — Martin Zinkevich, Google (developers.google.com) — terse, battle-tested production ML rules from Google.\n- Google Cloud Architecture Center: \"MLOps: Continuous delivery and automation pipelines in machine learning\" — reference architecture for CI/CD/CT maturity levels in ML systems.\n- Stanford CS 329S: Machine Learning Systems Design (Chip Huyen) — public course notes/syllabus; the course this board's core book grew out of.\n- Full Stack Deep Learning (fullstackdeeplearning.com) — production-focused course covering deployment, monitoring, and infra for ML teams.\n- Made With ML (madewithml.com, Goku Mohandas) — applied MLOps lessons with runnable code, from data to deployment.\n- Eugene Yan — applyingml.com — applied ML system design write-ups from practitioners at Amazon/Alibaba and other large-scale teams.\n- Evidently AI blog (evidentlyai.com) — deep dives specifically on ML monitoring, data drift, and concept drift detection in production.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "index": "b01"
    },
